## Introduction
Hydrogen peroxide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ is a green redox reagent with widespread applications in organic synthesis, medical treatment, jet cars and rockets, environmental remediation, and so on [l-4]. Currently, commercial $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is usually produced by the traditional anthraquinone (AQ) method, which utilizes Ni or Pd catalyst and $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ feed gases in the cycling redox processes of anthraquinone [5]. Very recently, more energy-efficient and environment-friendly method, such as photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production—sunlight as driving force as well as semiconductor as photocatalyst and oxygen and water as feedstock, has shown great promise in replacing the AQ method [5,6]. This is highly dependent on the performance of semiconductors for photocatalytic oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), i.e., light-absorption capacity, oxygenadsorption capacity, and band structure. For examples, mesoporous resin, ${\mathrm{BiVO}}_{4},$ carbon nitride $(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})$ , and covalent triazine frameworks (CTFs） have been adopted towards photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production [7-10].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are porous crystalline materials with periodic metallic ions/clusters’ arrangements interconnected by organic ligands, in addition to their multifarious roles in gas separation and storage, energy storage and conversion, sensor, and biomonitor mainly owing to their fascinating  structural  diversities [1l-15].  Among  MOFs' photocatalysts, Ti-based MOFs have aroused increasing interests owing to the low toxicity, rich reserves of titanium in earth, and unique photo-redox properties [l6]. The appealing Ti-based MOF， ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-MIL}}125$ (Material of the Institute Lavoisier, $\mathrm{\small{[Ti_{8}(O H)_{4}O_{8}(B D C-N H_{2})_{6}]}}$ ，herein denoted as aMIL),has been widely used in photocatalytic water splitting, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reaction $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR})$ ，and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ reduction reaction (NRR） [17-19]. Specially, the application of aMIL-based for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production under visible-light irradiation mainly originates from the introduction of secondary ligand [20,21], doping and the construction of heterojunction over pure aMIL [22-24]. In general, the catalytic reaction mainly takes place on the surface of the catalyst, so the level of exposed facets could determine the rate of catalysis reaction to a certain extent [8, 25]. Coincidentally, the degree of exposed facets for Ti-based MOFs could vary largely by changing the nucleation rate and the crystal growth process with the adjustable reactant concentration, the introduction of the structure-defining agent or surfactant, e.g., acetic acid or cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) [26-28], and the introduction of secondary ligand [21]. Importantly, Jiang and coworkers successively demonstrated that acetic acid acted as defect modulator could modulate the MOFs’ electronic and band structures, carrier concentration, porosity, and conductivity [29], and the variation of interfacial microenvironment induced with polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) for MOFs also presents a negative influence on the photocatalytic performance [30]. Combining the correlative density functional theory (DFT） calculations of aMIL [28],we attempted to regulate aMIL with acetic acid and explored the possible structure-activity relationship among facet, defect, and photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production performance.
Herein,we introduce acetic acid during the one-pot synthesis process, verifying the duet of facet and defect engineering over aMIL for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production. Nevertheless, the more introduction amount of acetic acid, the greater degree of $\{111\}$ facet and defect  engineering. {lll} facets  engineering by introducing modulator into aMIL may bring domino effects, for instance, changes of electron-hole pairs’ recombination rate, photoluminescence (PL） lifetime, charge-transfer resistance, and electron transferability, which thus improve the photocatalytic performance by exposing more active sites. Moreover, ligands acted as photoexcited sites were substituted with modulators, which could enlarge the surface area and pore volume, yet leading to the absorption wavelength with a blue shift to retard the reaction rate. The photocatalytic rate of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production shows a volcano-type trend with increasing {lll} facets and structural defect engineering. aMIL-3 photocatalyst could deliver $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production rate of $925.8\upmu\mathrm{molcdoth^{-1}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ under visible-light irradiation and a quantum yield of $1.08\%$ at $420\mathrm{nm}$
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Reagents
All chemicals were received from commercial resources and directly utilized without further purification. 2-Aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-BDC},\geq99\%)$ was purchased from Alfa Aesar (USA). Acetic acid(HOAc， $99.5\%$ )，titanium tetraisopropanolate $\mathrm{(C_{12}H_{28}O_{4}T i,}$ $99\%$ ， $^{\mathrm{N,N}}$ dimethylformamide $(\mathrm{DMF},\protect\geq99.5\%)$ ， anhydrous N,N-dimethylformamide $(99.8\%$ 。water $\leq50\ \mathrm{ppm},$ ， methanol $(\mathrm{MeOH},\ge99.5\%)$ , anhydrous methanol $(99.9\%$ water $\leq$ $50\mathrm{\ppm},$ ，acetonitrile $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN},\geq99\%)$ ，triethanolamine $(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{15}\mathrm{NO}_{3},$ $99\%-110\%$ )，tert-butyl alcohol $\mathrm{(C_{4}H_{10}O,~\geq~99\%)}$ ， benzoquinone $(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{2},99\%)$ , and 2-propanol $(\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}O},\geq99.7\%)$ were purchased from Admas Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. Superoxide dismutase $\left(\ge\ 3,000\right)$ units-mg-） was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich(USA). Hydrochloric acid (HCl, AR） was purchased from Beijing Tong Guang Fine Chemicals Company. Perchloric acid solution ( $\mathrm{\RClO_{4}},$ $99.999\%$ ),and oxo[5,10,15,20- tetra(4-pyridyl)porphinato]titanium(IV) $(\mathrm{[TiO(tpypH_{4})]^{4+}},>90\%)$ were purchased from Aladdin Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. Ultrapure water (Millipore, $18.2\:\mathrm{M}\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm})$ was used throughout all experiments.
## 2.2 Preparation of catalysts
## 2.3 Photocatalytic $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ production
Photocatalytic ORR (Scheme S2 in the ESM) was carried out in a top-irradiation Pyrex glass photocatalytic reactor (AuLight, Beijing) connected to a temperature-controlled (recycling cooling water, $15~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ ）closed gas circulation system. The reaction mixture was magnetically stirred at $800~\mathrm{rpm}$ under the irradiation of a 300 W Xe lamp (AuLight, CEL-HXF-300, Beijing) equipped with an optical filter ( $\lambda\geq420~\mathrm{nm}$ . Typically, $20~\mathrm{mg}$ photocatalyst was dispersed in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ acetonitrile-ultrapure water-triethanolamine solution $\mathrm{(v;v;v}=36{:}1{:}3$ ）by sonication for $10\mathrm{min}.$ the mixture was then transferred to the photocatalytic reactor, and pure $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was continuously bubbled at a flow rate of $100\ \mathrm{\mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ into the mixture in the dark for at least $30~\mathrm{min}$ to remove residual air and reach adsorption-desorption equilibrium. An aliquot of the solution was collected from the reaction mixture every ten minutes unless otherwise specified, and then analyzed with the spectroscopic titration method after the photocatalyst was filtered.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Structural characterizations
The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of as-synthesized samples are shown in Fig.1(a). All experimental PXRD patterns are in good agreement with the simulated one of pure aMIL, indicating that the introduction of modulator does not undermine the structural integrity of aMIL. It is worth noting that the diffraction intensity of (002), (222), and (004) crystallographic planes varies irregularly along with the gradual increased amounts of modulator, which could be related to the change of crystal shape [31]. $(h k l)$ and $\{h k l\}$ typify the Miller index and the group index of the crystal face [32], respectively. (222) and (004) crystallographic planes were selected to explore the diffraction intensity ratio of the $\{111\}$ and $\{001\}$ facets $(I_{(004)}/I_{(222)})$ and $I_{(004)}/I_{(222)}$ (Table Sl in the ESM) indicates the growth rate of {111) facet could be in sync with the gradually increased introduction of HOAc (Fig.S2 in the ESM). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) data (Fig.1(b)) are normalized according to the end weight of the anatase and rutile $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (PXRD patterns of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ are shown in Fig. S3 in the ESM), which combined with differential thermal analysis (DTA) data allows the assignment of three resolvable weight losses from 25 to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ 100 to ca. $390^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and 390 to ca. $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to the removals of absorbed water in MOFs' pores, structural water (i.e., dehydroxylation) and possible coordinated HOAc modulator and less $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ BDC ligand, and the combustion of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–{\mathrm{BDC}}$ ligand [33,34], respectively. The second-step weight losses are almost constant among all samples, implying that HOAc acts as a mild modulator that does not introduce significant structural defects in MOFs [35]. Intuitively, the replacement of dicarboxylate ligands with less  steric demanding  end-capped  monocarboxylate modulators should improve the surface area and pore volume of MOFs. Indeed, Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area and pore volume (Table S2 in the ESM) calculated from $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ isotherms show steady increase from 1,075.4 to $1{,}241{.}2\ \mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ and 0.4487 to $0.7002{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1};$ corroborating the above assumption. Moreover, all the isotherms (Fig.1(c)) belong to the reversible tetragonal plate (type I), consistent with the microporous nature [36]. In addition, pore size distribution (Fig.l(d)) does not change significantly based on DFT analysis, coherent with the conservation of structural integrity.Teller (BET） surface area and pore volume (Table S2 in the ESM) calculated from $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ isotherms  show steady increase from 1,075.4 to $1{,}241{.}2\ \mathrm{\m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and 0.4487 to $0.7002{\mathrm{~cm}}^{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1},$ corroborating the above assumption. Moreover, all the isotherms (Fig.1(c)) belong to the reversible tetragonal plate (type I), consistent with the microporous nature [36]. In addition, pore size distribution (Fig.l(d)) does not change significantly based on DFT analysis, coherent with the conservation of structural integrity.
## 3.2 Morphological characterizations
The changes of morphologies for aMIL (I), aMIL-1 (II), aMIL-2 (III),aMIL-3 (IV),and aMIL-4 (V) (Fig.2(a)） follow tetragonal plate (type I), circular-like plate (type II) to irregular truncated bipyramids with different perpendicular direction lengths (type IIV),which could correspond to the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Figs.2(b)-2(f)), and Fig. S4 in the ESM reveals the average lengthes of the side are ruleless, while the thicknesses are linearly distributed in the range of ca. 200 to $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ Further measurement and calculation of geometric area (Fig.S5 in the ESM) referring to the typical morphologies show that the average area proportion of $\{111\}$ facets $(P_{\{111\}})$ grows rapidly from 0 to $\sim88\%$ ，while that of $\{001\}$ facets $(P_{\{001\}})$ minishes from $100\%$ to $\sim12\%$ (Table S3 in the ESM),which could correspond well with the analysis result of PXRD. The faster growth rate of the crystal facet, the more difficult to expose it [37]. It is reasonable to assume that with increasing amount of acetic acid during the one-pot synthesis process with the same of reaction conditions, the growth of {1ll} facets slowed down until it was much lower than that of {001} facets. Meanwhile, the variation in morphology with increased equivalents of modulator has been found to be reminiscent of the adjustable reactant concentration and the addition of CTAB [26,28]. Distinct from these known methods where decreased surface areas are obtained, the surface areas in this work increase due to ligand replacement of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC by HOAc around the Ti nodes. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM） image of aMIL-3 in low resolution clearly shows the relatively well-defined polyhedral structure. TEM images with high resolution (Figs. 2(g) and $2\mathrm{(h)}\dot{}.$ ）and the corresponding elemental mappings (Fig.2(i)） demonstrate the uniform distribution of C, N, O, and Ti elements. X-ray energydispersive spectral (EDS) line scans (Figs. 2(j) and $2(\mathrm{k}),$ ）also confirm that the elements of C, N, O, and Ti are evenly distributed across the single polyhedral microstructure.
## 3.3 Defect detection
$\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR (NMR: nuclear magnetic resonance) spectra (Fig.3(a)) were acquired for all samples digested in $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ -containing $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ NaOH. There are two distinct sets of signals from downfield to upfield, which can be assigned to $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC ligand and HOAc modulator [29]. Notably, enhanced signal intensity of the OAc functional group can be observed accompanying the increase of modulator loading (Fig. 3(a), inset), implying the greater extent of ligand substitution by modulator evidenced the peak integral area of the $\mathrm{OAc^{-}}$ functional group (Fig.S6 in the ESM). Meanwhile, water contact angles measurement also is a compeling proof to find the hydrophilic nature of MOFs after introducing modulator. Water contact angles of the as-prepared sample plates were measured (Fig. S7 in the ESM), and the variation of water contact angle versus time is recorded in Fig.3(b). The elapsed times, referring to the time duration of water contact angle reducing from the initial state to $0{^\circ}_{:}$ are determined to be 3.5, 4.2, 4.5, 5.3, and $6.8\ \mathrm{min}$ for $\mathrm{\aMIL,}$ aMIL-1,aMIL-2,aMIL-3,and aMIL-4, respectively, with average reduction rate of water contact angle ca. 14.1,1l.4, 12.4, 12.5, and 6.0 deg-min-. The observed trend in hydrophilicity change could be rationalized by the loss of hydrophilic $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups  upon ligand  substitution  and concomitant porosity increase [38, 39]. The surface chemical states of all MOF samples were evaluated using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS） measurement in the region of $_{0-1,100\ \mathrm{eV}}$ Atomic concentrations for aMIL,aMIL-l,aMIL-2,aMIL-3,and aMIL-4 (Table S4 in the ESM) were obtained according to the XPS survey spectra shown in Fig.S8(a) in the ESM, indicating more Ti nodes were exposed in {lll} facets. As shown in the highresolution XPS spectra of Ti 2p (Fig.3(c)), there are two broad peaks located at about 464 and $458~\mathrm{eV}$ ， corresponding to $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ and
$2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ of $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ [40],respectively. The two peaks gradually shift to higher binding energy upon the increasing concentration of acetic acid, which are likely due to the displacement of electron-donating $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC ligand around the Ti-O cluster by OAc [4l, 42], while the missing electron-donating ligands could reduce catalytic activity of MOF [43]. The high-resolution XPS spectra of N 1s for all samples (Fig. 3(d)) exhibit peaks at 402.4 and $399.3~\mathrm{eV}_{:}$ which could be attributed to N of ${\mathrm{-NH{-}}}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups [44]. Highresolution XPS spectra of C 1s for all samples (Fig.S8(b) in the ESM)exhibit peaks at 288.8, 286.4,285.5,and $284.7~\mathrm{eV}$ 。which could be assigned to $\mathrm{C=C,}$ C-N,C-C, and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ bonds [44], respectively. The peaks (Fig.S8(c) in the ESM) centered at approximately 532.6, 531.5,and $530.4~\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to $-\mathrm{OH}$ group, $\mathrm{C=O},$ and Ti-O cluster [44].
To scrutinize the variations of local structures before and after introducing modulator, Ti K-edge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) and $k^{3}$ -weight Fourier-transformed extended X-ray absorption fine-structure (FT-EXAFS) spectra of the pristine aMIL and aMIL-3 (typically studied in this work) were characterized by synchrotron X-ray absorption spectra (XAS). Typical pre-edge features and white line peaks of Ti foil, aMIL, aMIL-3, and commercial P25 are shown in Fig.3(e). The absorption thresholds of both aMIL and aMIL-3 closely resemble the P25 standard sample, indicating the presence of $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ oxidation state. Notably, three pre-edge peaks denoted as a, b, and c were detected in P25 (Fig.3(e), inset) that arise from the hybridization between surrounding neighbors and the p or d orbitals of Ti atom, while only single pre-edge peak was observed for either aMIL or aMIL-3, which can be tentatively assigned to the Ti nodes $\mathrm{(TiO_{6})}$ [45]. The presence of distinct peaks in the post-edge region indicates the long- and medium-range ordered structures in aMIL and aMIL-3 [46]. $k^{3}$ -weight FT-EXAFS spectra (Fig.3(f)） show peaks at about 2.46, 1.18, 1.45 A for Ti foil, aMIL and aMIL-3, and P25,which could be assigned to the coordination of $\mathrm{Ti-Ti,}$ and Ti-O, respectively. Wavelet transform extended $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray absorption fine-structure (WT-EXAFS) spectra (Fig. $3(\mathrm{g)}{\cdot}$ ) can provide both kspace and radial distance resolution by separating backscattering atoms. The maximum intensities of Ti foil, $\mathrm{\aMIL,}$ aMIL-3,and P25 are 6.6, 3.9,4.6, and $5.5\mathrm{\AA}^{-1};$ which could be attributed to the coordination_of $\mathrm{Ti-Ti_{metallic}},$ $\mathrm{Ti{-}O_{\mathrm{{short}}},}$ $\mathrm{Ti{-}O_{\mathrm{{short}}},}$ and $\mathrm{Ti-O_{long},}$ respectively. The maximum intensity of aMIL-3 is larger than that of $\mathrm{\aMIL,}$ in agreement with the formation of TiO clusters with defects [47]. Overall, these results corroborate the retaining of $\mathrm{TiO}_{6}$ nodes within the framework upon introduction of defects.
## 3.4 Band structure characterizations
Optical properties and band structure for Ti-MOFs after introducing modulators were investigated by ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis）diffractionspectra(DRS)(Fig.4(a)）andthe corresponding Tauc plots (Fig.4(b)). Essentially, the increased ratio of {1l1} facet could make the absorption wavelength with a red shift [27], while defect could lead to the absorption wavelength with a blue shift resulting from the missing ligand (photoexcited site). There is no significant diference in UV-vis DRS for all samples, which could be the combination of facet and defect engineering. The band gaps of aMIL, aMIL-l, aMIL-2, aMIL-3, and aMIL-4 samples are 2.70, 2.71,2.71,2.7l, and $2.73\ \mathrm{eV}_{:}$ while Mott-Schottky plots (Fig.S10 in the ESM) show that the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO） potentials of the above samples are $-0.94,-0.95,-0.95,-0.97,$ and $-0.99{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (vs. $\mathrm{Ag/AgCl},$ $\mathrm{pH}=6.8\$ ), respectively. Therefore, the obtained LUMO potential $(E_{\mathrm{(Ag/AgCl,\pH=\6.8)}})$ ）is converted to $E_{\mathrm{(NHE,~pH~=~7)}}$ (NHE: normal hydrogen electrode) according to the followed Eq. (1) [48]
$$
E_{\mathrm{(NHE,pH=7)}}=E_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}-0.059\times\left(7-\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{electrolyte}}\right)+E_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}^{0}
$$
So, the LUMO potentials $(E_{\mathrm{(NHE,pH=7)}})$ of aMIL, aMIL-1, aMIL-2, aMIL-3, and aMIL-4 samples are accordingly determined to be -0.755,-0.765,-0.765, $-0.785$ 。and $-0.805\mathrm{~V},$ and the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) potentials $(E_{\mathrm{(NHE,pH=7)}})$ are 1.945,1.945,1.945,1.925,and 1.925 V.The band structure diagrams (Fig.4(c)) exhibit that the LUMO potentials of all the samples are more negative than $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ demonstrating that
ORR with $2e^{-}$ transfer is feasible thermodynamically. Besides, superoxide radicals $(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-})$ involved pathway $\left(\mathrm{O}_{2}\rightarrow\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ for ORR and water oxidation reaction (WOR） pathway $\mathrm{(H_{2}O/H_{2}O_{2})}$ are both accessible and cannot be ruled out [49].
## 3.5 Photocatalytic $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ production
To assess the feasibility of the WOR pathway in this photocatalytic system, the effect of different reaction conditions on the production rate of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is summarized in Fig.Sll in the ESM. Factors including light, photocatalyst, sacrificial agent, and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ feed gas are prerequisites for maximizing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production. $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production contribution from the WOR pathway is almost negligible, and the triethanolamine (TEOA) sacrificial agent could accelerate the transmission of electrons by restraining the recombination of electron-hole pairs. Thus, three types of sacrificial agents containing TEOA, MeOH, and 2-propanol (IPA) were added separately to the photocatalytic system where the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production rate with TEOA is the highest (Fig. Sl2(a) in the ESM). Meanwhile, the PL spectra (Fig.Sl2(b) in the ESM) were collected by simulating a similar photocatalytic system with different sacrificial agents. The PL intensity of TEOA system is lower than that of MeOH and IPA, supporting that TEOA could reduce the recombination rate of electrons and holes [50].
Based on the above control experiments, the performance of photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production for aMIL, aMIL-1, aMIL-2, aMIL3, and aMIL-4 photocatalysts was further assessed in $\mathrm{MeCN/H_{2}O/T E O A}$ (36:1:3 v/v/v, $40~\mathrm{mL}$ ）with $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ atmosphere under visible-light irradiation $\lambda\ge420~\mathrm{nm}$ ： $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production increases with a volcano-type trend (Figs. 5(a) and 5(b)) allowing the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production rates determined to be 456.7, 519.0,846.2, 925.8,and $636.1\ \upmu\mathrm{mol.h^{-1}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ for aMIL, aMIL-1, aMIL-2, aMIL-3, and aMIL-4, respectively. While the degree of {lll} facets up to $89\%$ means more structural defects exist for aMIL-4, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production rates would be retarded. Moreover, the differences of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ decomposition (Fig. S13 in the ESM) among the samples are not significant. Apparently, aMIL-3 photocatalyst delivers the highest ORR activity, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production rate of aMIL-3 photocatalyst is 2.03-fold compared with aMIL photocatalyst. The result of cycling stability (Fig.5(c)) demonstrates that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production rate of aMIL-3 photocatalyst  exhibited  well repeatability during a $6\mathrm{~h~}$ experiment. The aMIL-3 sample after cycling test was monitored by PXRD parttern (Fig.Sl4 in the ESM), XPS spectra (Fig.S15 in the ESM),SEM images, and EDS line scans (Fig.Sl6 in the ESM), indicating that aMIL-3 possesses good structural stability after comparing these results with pristine aMIL-3 sample. Good correlation between the apparent quantum efficiency (AQE) and the absorption spectrum (Fig. 5(d)) implies the occurrence of a light-driven reaction. The photocatalyst $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production rate and AQE value $(1.08\%)$ of aMIL-3 are comparable to the reported excellent photocatalysts, highlighting the feasibility of structural defects engineering in ameliorating photocatalytic performance (Table S5 in the ESM).
## 3.6 Photocatalytic mechanism
To better understand the reaction pathway in this photocatalytic system, three different scavengers tert-butyl alcohol (TBA), superoxide dismutase (SOD), and benzoquinone (BQ） were selected to trap the hydroxyl radical (-OH) and superoxide radical $(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-})$ [51]. Figure 6(a) shows that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ production rates of aMIL3 photocatalyst drop drastically in presence of $\mathrm{{\calSOD/BQ}},$ implying that $\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ intermediate is likely involved during ORR. In addition, DMPO spin-trapping electron spin resonance (ESR) test was conducted to monitor the $\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ signal in a simulative photocatalytic system containing $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated MeCN/TEOA (v/v,13:l）under dark or visible-light irradiation( $\lambda\ge420~\mathrm{nm},$ .ESR results (Fig. $\mathsf{6(b)}.$ ） confirm the presence of $\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ species under visible-light irradiation, thus a two-step single-electron reaction pathway $(\mathrm{O}_{2}\to$ $\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\to\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot},$ ）could be proposed. Meanwhile, ESR spectra (Fig. $6(\mathrm{c}))$ of aMIL-3 under dark and irradiation substantiate the existence of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ on the basis of the g value under visible-light irradiation [19]. The catalytic mechanism for photoinduced ORR is depicted in Fig.6(d). Based on the ligand-to-cluster charge transfer (LCCT)mechanism [52],electrons (e-） excited by visiblelight irradiation were transferred from ligands to clusters along with the generation of highly reactive $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ that reduces the trapped $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to form $\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ intermediate followed by another one-electron reduction to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and regenerate $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ . It is noteworthy that the photogenerated holes $\left(\mathrm{h}^{+}\right)$ were consumed through the oxidation reaction with TEOA,which acts as a sacrificial agent and electron donor [53].
